<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Nizari Ismailis of Syria</h1><p class="st-meta">1132 CE – 1273 CE (approx.) · capital Masyaf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Syrian branch of the Nizari Ismailis, the 'Assassins' of Crusader legend, who ruled a network of mountain castles around Masyaf.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> missionaries sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> first worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> but were driven out by massacres. From 1132 they acquired castles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-coastal-mountain-range">Jabal Bahra</a>, the coastal mountains of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadmus">Qadmus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kahf-castle">Kahf</a> and in 1140-1141 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masyaf">Masyaf</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The castles formed a small state led by a chief missionary appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Rashid al-Din Sinan</a>, the original Old Man of the Mountain, led it from about 1162 to 1193, making it largely independent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizaris</a> used targeted killings by devotees against powerful enemies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a> in 1192, and twice attempted to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. They also paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitallers</a> at times.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in 1256, the Syrian castles survived, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> took them one by one between 1270 and 1273. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> community remained in the region, notably around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamiyah">Salamiyah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masyaf">Masyaf</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1132 CE – 1273 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.23%;width:2.81%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1132 CE – 1273 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Masyaf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rashid al-Din Sinan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Nizari Ismaili Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Syrian Assassins, Nizari state in Syria</dd></dl></aside></div>

